#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "90308076-a0ef-5e6a-900d-2ef4c8107907" :question/math-academy-id "e-5466" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}$?" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T$. So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} \\cdot T \\cdot v &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nv &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} &= (1)/(-15 - (-20))\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "7969785b-fc71-50f4-9d73-da5599630b6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5467" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$. The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10)$, and $(-4,-3)$. Which of the following are vertices of $S$?\n\n1. $(1,0)$\n2. $(4,-3)$\n3. $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT \\cdot X &= Y \\\\\nT \\cdot X &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T$. So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} \\cdot T \\cdot X &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nX &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{-1} &= (1)/(4 - (-3))\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= T^{-1} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e9af48-e483-5310-bed2-e830dc1d7767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091524} {:answer/id #uuid "c90afd80-494f-5ba0-aff1-436b856757b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091525} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e0691d-3105-533d-a1d4-e7d31ca991d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091526} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8e76bd-efd3-5f0a-81cd-ebb17fd9aa09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091527} {:answer/id #uuid "4853fd0b-1ff9-54ba-98b7-cf78a1d1c24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e0691d-3105-533d-a1d4-e7d31ca991d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091526} :answer-field/id #uuid "151ddac5-9f50-5d1c-b74e-1a1f348d9840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091523}] :question/id #uuid "867b7546-7038-557a-b857-b13076be50c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-9265" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram $P^{′}$ under the action of $T$. The parallelogram $P^{′}$ has vertices $(0, 2), (-3, 3), (12, 4)$, and $(7, 3)$. Which of the following are vertices of $P$?\n\n1. $(-2, 2)$\n2. $(1,-1)$\n3. $(4, 4)$"}]}
